#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pass 59 - latency was measured and reported, never gated.

    .venv/bin/python patches/quality_pass59.py            apply
    .venv/bin/python patches/quality_pass59.py --check    verify, change nothing

Pass 58 built a loop that gates correctness. Asked to use it to improve
performance, the honest answer was that it could not: `scripts/timings.py` has
printed a per-stage latency profile since pass 30-odd, and nothing ever compared
one run to the last. An agent told to make the agent faster could trade
correctness for milliseconds, or spend 40% more time for no measurable gain, and
every gate would stay green.

So timings.py gains `--json`, `agent_loop.py gate` reads it, and six numbers are
gated - all lower-is-better:

    worst_python_ms   llm_best_ms            llm_model_ms
    total_best_ms     llm_prompt_tokens      llm_completion_tokens

Per-question keys were the obvious design and are not used: six questions times
three numbers is eighteen gates that all move together, and a gate nobody reads
is a gate nobody maintains. These six have DIFFERENT fixes, which is the only
reason to separate them - a slow Python path is SQL or the event fold, a slow
model stage is generation length, and a grown prompt is retrieval pulling more
than it needs.

timings.py JOINS THE LOCK

It now feeds a gate, so an agent that can edit it can report whatever
milliseconds it likes. The lock covers six files rather than five, and the
baseline was retaken.

THE THRESHOLDS ARE MEASURED, NOT GUESSED

Three consecutive runs on an idle L40S, before choosing any number:

    search question      1797 / 1799 / 1795 ms      0%
    prompt_tokens        1146 / 1146 / 1146         0%
    completion_tokens        99 /   99 /   99       0%
    python paths >6ms                            1-2%
    the 2ms path                                  20%   (0.4ms absolutely)

So the gate allows 15% or 5ms on times, 10% on tokens - about ten times the
observed noise. Deliberately loose: a latency gate that cries wolf gets switched
off, and the thing worth catching is a stage that doubles, not one that costs 3%.
The 5ms floor exists so the 2ms question's sub-millisecond wobble cannot fail a
run.

Verified against ten synthetic cases rather than assumed: +4ms passes, +14%
passes, +16% fails, a doubled model stage fails, a prompt grown to 1400 tokens
fails, a 5% prompt growth passes, everything 30% faster passes, and a metric
that disappears fails rather than being read as a pass.

WHAT THE PROFILE SAYS TO OPTIMISE

    python paths      2-62 ms    no model call at all
    search question   1797 ms    model 1453, rerank 108, embed 0 (cached), rest 235
                                 prompt 1146 tokens -> 99 generated, 68 tok/s

Five of six questions never reach a model, so a slow one there is SQL, not the
GPU. For the sixth, generation is 1453 of 1797 ms and completion_tokens is 99
against a max_tokens of 400 - the answer is that length, not truncated, so
making it faster means making it shorter, which is a quality decision rather
than a free win.

And the reranker is not the place to cut: the ablation measures +5.0 points of
recall@6 and +0.018 MRR for +0.01s per query over 120 queries. It earns its
latency, and that has been measured twice because an earlier run used 40 queries
and wrongly concluded it did not.

A truncated answer is also now surfaced: finish_reason == "length" prints a
warning, because an answer that stopped early is short rather than fast, and
timings.py is the only thing that sees it.
"""
from __future__ import annotations
import hashlib, pathlib, sys

ROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
CHECK = "--check" in sys.argv


def sha(b: str) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(b.encode()).hexdigest()


